// Generated by Claude (Opus 5.5), 2026-10-25
// spsc-bench.cpp
// Latency of RingBuffer push and pop with ITCH traffic, received over UDP or replayed from memory.
//
//   udp:     itch-replay --UDP--> ItchParser.on_tick -> push (producer core) ==RingBuffer==> pop loop (consumer core)
//   memory:  ticks decoded from the ITCH file up front -> push (producer core) ==RingBuffer==> pop loop (consumer core)
//
// Easiest: ./spsc-bench.sh (builds one binary per variant, runs it, compares).
// By hand (from src/feed-handler/claude):
//   g++-14 -std=c++23 -O2 -march=native -DNDEBUG spsc-bench.cpp -lpthread -o ../../../build/spsc-bench
//   ../../../build/spsc-bench --mode e2e --out result.json &   # prints "ready" once bound
//   ../../../build/itch-replay --file ~/itch-data/01302019.NASDAQ_ITCH50 --speed 10
// or, with no network:
//   ../../../build/spsc-bench --source memory --itch-file ~/itch-data/01302019.NASDAQ_ITCH50 --speed 100
//
// The ring buffer under test comes from SPSC_HEADER (default ../spsc.hpp). Any header
// that defines RingBuffer<T, N> with bool push(const T&) and std::optional<T> pop() works.
// SPSC_BUCKETS sets N (default 65536, as in main.cpp).
//
// Modes:
//   e2e      No timer around the calls. Each pushed tick carries the TSC read just before
//            push; the consumer reads it again after pop. Rows: e2e (push to pop, ns).
//            The fairer way to compare strategies: nothing serialises the threads.
//   percall  Every push and pop is timed with lfence/rdtsc (cycle-clock.hpp), net of timer
//            overhead. Rows: push, pop (returned a tick), pop_empty, and e2e (which here
//            includes the timers). The fences slow both threads down, so the interleaving
//            is not the same as in e2e mode.
// A push that finds the queue full counts in "full" and is retried until it succeeds;
// only the first attempt is in the push row, and e2e includes the wait.
//
// Memory source (--source memory): the --from/--messages window of --itch-file is decoded
// with the same rules as ItchParser before the run, so the producer only paces and pushes.
// Two ways to make the queue busy without a network in the way:
//   bursts   --speed X replays the tick timestamps X times faster (0: flat out), and
//            --consumer-work-ns N makes the consumer spin N ns after each pop. In busy
//            periods the consumer falls behind and the queue fills; it drains when the
//            traffic eases. Tune X and N so the peak stays under capacity.
//   fixed    --depth D --period-ns T pre-fills D ticks, then the producer pushes and the
//            consumer pops one tick every T ns on the same TSC schedule, so occupancy stays
//            at D for the whole run. Only steady-state operations are measured: pushes after
//            the pre-fill, and pops of ticks D to N-D.
// --repeat R replays the decoded window R times in one run (memory source), so the traffic
// lasts long enough to sample with perf without decoding the file again.
//
// Profiling: the threads are named spsc-producer and spsc-consumer. --perf-ctl CTL,ACK talks
// to perf's control FIFOs (perf stat|record -D -1 --control fifo:CTL,ACK): counting is
// enabled right before the traffic starts and disabled after the consumer has finished, so
// decoding the ITCH file and the setup are not counted.
//
// At high occupancy the e2e row is mostly queueing (about occupancy x time per tick); the
// push and pop rows from percall mode are what reflect the queue itself.
//
// Occupancy is worked out after the run from the push and pop timestamps of every tick, so
// the queue sees no extra shared counter: for each push, the ticks pushed before it minus
// the ticks popped before it.
//
// The queue lives in .bss, so its pages are not mapped until first written: without a
// pre-touch, the first lap takes one page fault (about 2 us) every 4 KB. One push/pop lap
// on the producer core before "ready" maps them all. Page faults during the run are printed.
//
// Output: --out FILE in Google Benchmark's JSON shape, so bench-compare.py reads it.
// Exit status 3 if the run is not valid: lost or malformed packets, or the consumer
// did not get exactly the ticks the producer pushed.

#include "../tick.hpp"
